Yes. I remember the core idea quite well.

The framework you're exploring is to model investigations as graph data structures, rather than as prompt chains or decision trees. The goal is to make investigations:

Structured and parameterized.

More reliable by constraining the LLM to narrowly defined reasoning steps.

Replayable, auditable, and incrementally refinable.

The medical diagnosis example has been your first test domain. Along the way we've discussed several important ideas:

The graph is not really a tree. Multiple complaints motivate the same actions, actions produce multiple observations, and later evidence can point back to earlier hypotheses.

Early in the investigation you have things like:

complaints

investigative actions

observations

Later you introduce hypotheses, but we realized some of your initial "hypotheses" weren't actually hypotheses yet—they were more like intermediate findings or problem states.

We distinguished exploratory actions (e.g. measuring vitals) from discriminating actions whose purpose is specifically to distinguish between competing hypotheses.

We also talked about the graph eventually reaching some form of conclusion rather than just stopping after generating hypotheses.

More recently you decided not to spend forever polishing the Mermaid diagrams because they were serving their purpose. We also discussed whether to model another domain, and my recommendation was essentially:

Go deep enough in medicine to discover the abstractions, then validate the model with one substantially different domain (debugging software, fraud investigation, root cause analysis, etc.) rather than building many medical examples.

I also remember that your broader vision isn't "medical diagnosis." It's a general investigation framework where an LLM reasons over a typed graph of entities and relationships instead of free-form chain-of-thought.

Personally, I think you've reached the point where the interesting questions are no longer about Mermaid syntax or graph layout. The interesting questions are things like:

What are the fundamental node types?

What are the allowed edge types?

What invariants does every investigation satisfy?

How does the graph evolve over time?

How can an LLM be constrained to only perform legal graph transformations?

Those feel like the next layer of the work, and they're much more likely to determine whether the idea generalizes than producing another large example graph.


